It's more than semantics, because the two models predict different behavior. Try this:

```java
static void reset(List<String> list) {
    list = new ArrayList<>();
    list.add("new");
}

List<String> mine = new ArrayList<>(List.of("old"));
reset(mine);
System.out.println(mine); // [old]
```

With true pass-by-reference, the caller's `mine` would now be the new list. It isn't, because only a copy of the reference was passed. This distinction does come up in interviews and in real bugs, like expecting a method to replace a caller's object.

