```js
function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let timer;
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}
```

Usage:

```js
const onResize = debounce(() => console.log(window.innerWidth), 200);
window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
```

Each call resets the timer, so `fn` runs only after `wait` ms pass with no new calls. I used a regular `function` for the wrapper so `this` gets passed through when it's used as a method or event handler. The arrow function inside `setTimeout` keeps that same `this`.

